WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.941
Idea. Particles in a liquid or a gas move about all the time, in random directions. Concentration means how many particles are in a space. Diffusion is the net movement of particles from a high concentration to a low concentration. Net means overall.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:31.470
The difference in concentration between two places is the concentration gradient. In diffusion, particles move down the gradient, like a ball rolling downhill. The cell spends no energy on it.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:46.611
Method. There are three ways to cross a membrane. A membrane is the thin layer around a cell. The first is diffusion: high to low concentration. It is passive, which means the cell spends no energy.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:01:00.531
The second is osmosis. Osmosis moves water only. Water crosses a partially permeable membrane. Partially permeable means it lets small molecules like water through, but not large ones.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:14.743
For osmosis, use water potential. A dilute solution has a high water potential. A concentrated solution has a low water potential. Water moves from high to low water potential.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:39.500
The third is active transport. It moves particles from a low to a high concentration. That is against the gradient, like pushing a ball uphill. So it needs energy, as ATP. ATP is a molecule that carries energy. It is made in respiration, the reaction that releases energy from glucose. Carrier proteins in the membrane carry the particles across.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:55.355
So the method is: name the process. Give the direction compared with the gradient. Then add its special idea: no energy for diffusion, water and the membrane for osmosis, energy as ATP for active transport.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:11.173
Worked example. Now a real exam question. It says molecules move in and out of cells in three ways. Part b asks: give a definition of active transport. It is worth three marks, so plan three ideas.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:24.623
Idea one is the direction. Outside the cell the concentration is low. Inside it is high. Active transport moves molecules from low to high: against the concentration gradient.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:33.090
Idea two: this needs energy. Idea three: the energy is in the form of ATP, made by respiration.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:40.816
A spare idea, in case one is not given credit: carrier proteins in the membrane carry the molecules across.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.977
Here is the full answer. It is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:56.664
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say active transport moves particles down the gradient. It moves them up, against it.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:07.019
Another mistake is to say osmosis moves particles or solutes. Osmosis moves only water, across a partially permeable membrane.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:18.169
Your turn. A red blood cell is put into pure water. The cell swells. Name the process that makes it swell, and define it. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:29.147
Answer. The process is osmosis. Pure water has the highest water potential. The inside of the red blood cell has a lower water potential.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:39.511
So water moves into the cell, from high to low water potential, across the partially permeable cell membrane. The water goes in, and the cell swells.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:51.217
So, every time: name the process. Give the direction compared with the gradient. Then add its special idea: water and the membrane, or energy as ATP.
